the World Health Organization has  praised China's attempts to contain the  coronavirus the country was quick to  seal off who Bay province where the  outbreak began closing factories and  banning travel and the impact is clear  this shows the daily new cases in who  base since the beginning the numbers  that were once in the thousands are now  in the hundreds and the strict  quarantine measures have stopped the  virus spreading to the rest of China  numbers there are comparatively low but  the virus did spread abroad slowly at  first but now as this line shows the  growth in daily cases is much larger  outside China that comparison is even  clearer when shown as a proportion of  new cases over the last three weeks  China makes up a dwindling share of the  total in Europe the main focus is Italy  which has at least eight times the  number of cases of any other EU country  and a fatality rate of 3.2 percent well  above the global average of one percent  one reason could be that almost a  quarter of Italy's population is aged  over 65 and the elderly are of course at  the highest risk of serious disease  researchers at Imperial College London  think under reporting of mild cases  could also be a factor officially there  are just over 2,500 cases but the real  number could be between 50,000 and  100,000 because so many people have mild  symptoms and taking that into account  would bring the fatality rate down Italy  has though exported the virus to a  number of countries including Spain  where as of lunchtime today at least 18  or is 198 cases had traveled to Italy 18  of Sweden's 35 cases can be traced to  the country and at least 22 of the uk's  87 cases were imported from there the  outbreak in the UK is several weeks  behind  Italy's but we do now have  person-to-person spread and the concern  is we may soon have an epidemic here
 

